1. Network settings for Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 instance:

   a. Ensure that your EC2 instance has the correct security groups in place. By default, the `default` security group should allow incoming traffic from ports 80 or 443 (if you're using HTTPS), and 22 (if you're connecting via SSH). If you have a custom security group, make sure it allows inbound traffic on the appropriate ports (80 or 443 for web traffic, and 22 for SSH) and that your instance is associated with that security group. B. If your EC2 instance was launched with a private IP (`primaryPrivateIpAddress`), create an Elastic IP (EIP) and associate it with the instance's network interface (`networkInterfaces.0.interfaceId`).

   C. Update your `config/database.php` file with the sandbox database details that have been configured on your EC2 instance. D. In your `app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php` file, add the following code to set up the environment variables for Dusk:

   ```php
   use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Config;
   use Laravel\Dusk\ChromeDriver;

   class AppServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
   {
       protected $deferredServices = [
           'chrome.driver' => function () {
               $chromeDriver = (string) Config::get('dusk.driver', 'chrome');
               $options = Config::get('dusk.chrome.driver_options', []);

               if ($chromeDriver === 'chrome') {
                   $browser = new ChromeDriver(env('LARASON_BROWSER_PATH', '/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable'));
               } else {
                   $browser = new ChromeDriver('/usr/bin/chromium-browser');
               }

               if (! empty($options)) {
                   $browser->setBrowserExecutablePath($options['path']);

                   foreach ($options['args'] as $arg) {
                       $browser->addOption(new ChromeOption([
                           'name' => $arg['key'],
                           'value' => $arg['value'],
                       ]));
                   }
               }

               return $browser;
           },
       ];

       // ...
   }
   ```

   Here, we've added the `LARASON_BROWSER_PATH` environment variable with a default value of `/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable`, which corresponds to the default installation location of Chromium-based browsers on Ubuntu-based EC2 instances. Update it with the actual path to your browser's executable if it's different. E. Add the following code to your `config/dusk.php` file to specify the url of your web application and set up the appropriate environment variables:

   ```php
   'url' => env('LARASON_SITE_URL', 'http://your-domain-or-ip-address'),

   'options' => [
       // ...
       'args' => [
           [
               'key' => 'user-data-dir',
               'value' => '/home/username/.config/google-chrome',
           ],
       ],
   ],

   'extensions' => [
       // ...
   ],

   'features' => [
       // ...
   ],
   ```

   Replace `your-domain-or-ip-address` with the Elastic IP that you have associated with your EC2 instance's network interface (`networkInterfaces.0.networkInterfaceId`). You can also specify the directory for storing Dusk's user data under `options.args[0]`. F. In your `dusk.conf` file, update the environment variables as follows:

   ```ini
   description: "Starts the Dusk browser for testing"

   env LARASON_DIR="/path/to/your/laravel/application"

   env LARASON_SITE_URL=http://your-domain-or-ip-address

   env LARASON_HEADLESS=true

   env LARASON_DRIVER=headless-chrome

   env LARASON_BROWSER_PATH=/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable

